Here's a potentially less complex thought-experiment that might prove 
equivalence, now that I think about it.

Take the set of all numbers between 0.1 and 1, of finite or infinite length.

Now, assign each of these numbers (let us say x) to a positive integer 
equal to x * 10^(the number of digits in x).

Then, there is a one-to-one relation between the set of all such numbers 
and the set of positive integers not divisible by 10^n, where n is a 
positive integer.

Now, the set of all numbers between 0 and 0.1 is equal to the set of 
(all pre-existing numbers in the above set multiplied by 10^(1/n), where 
n is a positive integer).

Assign each of the numbers in this set (y) to a positive integer equal 
to y * 10^(n + the number of digits in y). Then there is a one-to-one 
relation between the set of all such numbers and the set of positive 
integers divisible by 10^n, where n is a positive integer.

Hence we can establish a one-to-one relation between the two sets by 
dividing each set into two subsets.

